# Nested git repositories scripts/agent-sandbox.zsh accepts under this project — one
# per line, the path (relative to the project root) of the directory that
# holds the nested .git; zsh glob patterns are allowed; `#` starts a comment.
#
# Why: a submodule entry in the index makes the host's git read the nested
# repository's config, including core.fsmonitor, which runs as you. The
# nested config is writable from inside the container (it is not under the
# read-only overlays), so `agent-sandbox` refuses to attach while an unlisted nested
# repository exists. Worktrees under .claude/worktrees and .worktrees, and
# node_modules, are never checked.
#
# This file is read on the HOST and sits under the read-only .devcontainer
# overlay, so the agent cannot list its own repositories. Per-project:
# sync-harness.sh copies it only when missing. A listed repository is one
# you trust: on the host, never run git, a git-aware prompt or an IDE inside
# it (its config and hooks are writable from the container). `git config
# diff.ignoreSubmodules all` on the host only keeps plain status/diff/log
# from spawning git in it by accident; it is a reporting setting, not a
# boundary. For untrusted code prefer a clone the host never sees (a Docker
# volume), see README "Auditing another repository".
#
# vendor/some-lib
# audits/*
